  "worked_solution": "To solve this inequality using the graphical method, we first need to move all of the numbers to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & <3x \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y={x}^{2}-3x$ lies below the $x$-axis. Let's find the roots of the parabola by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-3x & =0 \\\\ x(x-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the roots are $x=0$ and $x=3,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004892/assets/a341979142ef569d7ba4ec5b44178e8b4e4374fa9294e4664ae0f0008b76caab.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving\n \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-3x<0,\n$$\n\n we look for the part of the parabola that is below the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $0<x<3.$ \n\nUsing interval notation, we write $x\\in (0,3).$",
